 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Kai Nesselrath signaled his new day at Carven by alluding to the sheets of a bed in the morning. It was a subtle, intimate idea that took shape as dresses folding over along the edges or falling from the body in gestures of deft draping. </p><p>With this debut show, the designer, a longtime tailoring alum at Saint Laurent, seems to have opted for baby steps more than big leaps. But what already stands out is the easy-breeziness he’s laying out for us. He revisited the polish of the ’50s and ’60s—a golden Carven era—as pale blue and pink ensembles with a fresher, youthful tilt (polo and pajama tops), while creating more abstracted versions that were cloudlike and bloomer’d. Imagining the Carven woman as a ‘doer,’ he alluded to workwear standbys, only his versions were given cocoon, couture-adjacent cuts. </p><p>He showed maxi-length coats, maxi-length apron dresses and a maxi dark denim skirt that were complete looks, yet there were also miniskirts with belt loops sans belts and backs flaps left open that landed more spontaneous. He produced witty jewelry: pencil shavings as golden earrings that bent and zigzagged, along with gleaming pencil hair accessories (making one nostalgic for the HB). </p><p>“It’s learning bits here and there, which is actually terribly fun,” said Nesselrath from his office that was filled with abundant floral deliveries still wrapped in paper. Among the senders of these pre-show bouquets was Chemena Kamali (a former Saint Laurent colleague) and Drew Henry (who had just made his own debut at Courrèges). </p><p>For any designer entering the house of Carven, one of the talking points—a challenge for better or worse—is invariably that Marie-Louise Carven didn’t leave the kind of heuristics that designers at other Paris houses inherit. Nesselrath said he immersed himself in the archives, and his moodboard suggested how intentional he was about piecing together a puzzle of old and new. He turned her recurring use of a deep green into a stripe of varying widths that offers branding possibility to play with going forward. He said he was adamant about touches of hand craft, hence a specific stitch known as the “vous et moi” [you and me] that bordered some of the softer, draped pieces, as well as a more modern twist on a flower jacquard and repeated tufts of embroidery that brought texture to a pair of white trousers. </p><p>Whether top of mind of not, as summers get hotter, Nesselrath’s focus on lightness will serve well when women are seeking clothes that breathe without veering too undressed. He noted in his personal statement the importance of breathing—how “allowing people to take a moment to breathe is giving people freedom; it’s taking a step back to reflect.”</p><p>There was an interstitial air to today’s show, as though Nesselrath has much more up his sleeve. He certainly exudes charm in spades, and if he can transfer some more of that—perhaps even via the models next time—Carven will be up and running.</p></div>",
